<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick III, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1415">1415</a>–<a href="/years/1493">1493</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick III was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> duke of Inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> who became King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1440 and the last emperor crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in 1452. In 1453 he confirmed the forged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/privilegium-maius">Privilegium Maius</a>, giving the Habsburgs the title of archduke. His long reign was beset by rebellion and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a>, but he raised loyal lords to higher rank and arranged his son Maximilian's Burgundian marriage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Frederick III, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="487" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Burgkmair the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_Burgkmair_d._%C3%84._005.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was born on 21 September 1415 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, the eldest son of Duke Ernest of Inner Austria and Cymburgis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>. His father died in 1424, making the nine-year-old duke of Inner Austria, which comprised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, with his uncle Frederick IV of Austria as regent. Relatives kept him from ruling until 1435, and his younger brother Albert VI began a long rivalry by asserting a claim to co-rule. In 1436 he made a pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. From 1439 he was regent for Sigismund in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and for his nephew Ladislaus the Posthumous in the duchy of Austria. On 2 February 1440 the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and unanimously elected him King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1452 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to receive his bride, Eleanor of Portugal, and to be crowned emperor. He was crowned with the German crown as King of Italy on 16 March, married that afternoon, and crowned emperor on 19 March. He was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His reign of 41 years was the longest in the empire's history. His style was hesitant, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Enea Silvio Piccolomini</a>, later Pope Pius II, said he wanted to conquer the world while remaining seated. Historians now see his delaying as a way of coping with far-flung possessions.</p><p>He prevailed over his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rivals largely by outliving them. He gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> from Ladislaus in 1457 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a> from Albert VI, who besieged him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1462. He failed against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, who defeated him in 1485 and held Vienna until his death. Frederick fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> and later Linz. Ottoman raids struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, prompting the Carinthian Peasant Revolt of 1478. In 1486 Maximilian was elected king in his father's lifetime, and he joined the empire's reform first under pressure in Frederick's last years. His personal motto was the string A.E.I.O.U., which he never explained.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frederick's son Maximilian married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a>, heir to the Burgundian lands, which gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> saying that others should wage wars while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> should marry. In 1493 his left leg was amputated at Linz on 8 June, a well-documented medieval operation, and he died on 19 August 1493, aged 77. His tomb in St Stephen's Cathedral, made by Nikolaus Gerhaert, is among the most important sculptures of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><p>The Austrian historian Adam Wandruszka calls him the true founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> imperial position, and German historians tend to be more critical than Austrian ones. He collected 110 books that formed the core of the later Bibliotheca Regia, forerunner of the Austrian National Library.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick III, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its most serious defeat came in the Old Zürich War of 1440–1446, when Zürich, in conflict with Schwyz over the inheritance of the last Count of Toggenburg, allied with the <b>Habsburg King Frederick III</b> against the other confederates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1453 <b>Emperor Frederick III</b>, himself a Habsburg, confirmed the forgery, giving the rulers of Austria the rank of archduke and special privileges.</blockquote><a href="/state/archduchy-of-austria">Archduchy of Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>After a century of feuds among Frisian chieftains, Ulrich Cirksena united most of East Frisia, and in 1464 <b>Emperor Frederick III</b> made him an imperial count.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-east-frisia">County of East Frisia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1474 <b>Emperor Frederick III</b> raised Holstein to a duchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holstein">County of Holstein</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1453 <b>Emperor Frederick III</b>, himself a Habsburg, confirmed the Privilegium Maius, raising Austria to an archduchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-austria">Duchy of Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1474 <b>Emperor Frederick III</b> raised Holstein, a fief of the empire, to a duchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-holstein">Duchy of Holstein</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Linz, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.27,11.39&amp;year=1465" aria-label="Innsbruck on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4817 505 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick III, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5267 775h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5267 775h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.27,11.39&amp;year=1465">What was here in 1465? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
